// Saying that a contract declined.
//
// An OPTIONAL half, carried only by a card that declares a `requires`, an
// `ensures` or an `invariant`.
//
// A rule that does not hold makes the transition not happen, and that much the
// compiled backend always did. What it did in SILENCE was say so: the
// interpreter reports a `Refusal` — the handler, the rule, the state, and the
// sentence the rule's own `format` writes — and a compiled guest has no channel
// back to the host shaped like a refusal record.
//
// It has `control.log`, which is the least a guest can ask for: no capability
// gates it and the WIT calls it "immediate and always legal". So the LINE the
// interpreter prints crosses, and the record does not. That is a smaller thing
// than parity and a much larger thing than nothing — a card author watching a
// handler quietly do nothing now reads which rule turned it away.
//
// The line is `core/warn.mbt`'s, verbatim, so the two backends say the same
// sentence about the same refusal:
//
//   contract: `publish` declined — its precondition `hasTitle` does not hold
//   contract: `publish` declined — its precondition `hasTitle` does not hold: the title is empty
//
// Both halves of it are interned by the generator, because both are known when
// it runs: only whether there IS a sentence, and what it says, is not.

// The line the generated half is about to hand `ctl_log`. A global rather than
// a return value because the import takes a (pointer, length) pair and a Wax
// function answers one thing.
let tc_log_msg: &?utf8_string = null;

// `ok` is 0 when the rule declares no `format`, or when evaluating it failed —
// which is the same `""` the interpreter's `sentence()` answers for both. A
// refusal that cannot describe itself is still a refusal.
fn tc_contract_msg(
    head: &utf8_string,
    head_colon: &utf8_string,
    ok: i32,
    sentence: &jv_value,
) {
    if ok == 0 {
        tc_log_msg = head;
        return;
    }
    tc_log_msg = utf8_concat(head_colon, tc_display(sentence));
}

fn tc_log_ptr() -> i32 { tc_str_out(tc_log_msg!) }

fn tc_log_len() -> i32 { utf8_byte_length(tc_log_msg!) }
